==English==
{{wikipedia}}
===Etymology===
1538 as noun, 1660 as verb. By reduplication. Perhaps from a dialectical word or Scandinavian; compare Old Norse {{term|flim||[[lampoon]], [[mockery]]|lang=non}}.<ref>{{R:Online Etymology Dictionary}}</ref>

===Pronunciation===
* {{rhymes|æm}}
* {{IPA|/ˈflɪmflæm/}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun|sg=[[flim]]-[[flam]]}} (Not recorded with this spelling in major dictionaries; usually spelled [[flimflam]].)

# Misinformation; [[bunkum]]; false information presented as true.
#: ''Most reports of supernatural occurrences turn out to be '''flim-flam''' when carefully investigated.''
# [[confidence game]], [[con game]]

====Derived terms====
* [[flim-flam man]]: Confidence trickster
* [[flam]]

